as children return to school remotely or  in person there is a growing concern  about their mental and emotional health  last may 29 percent of parents surveyed  said their child was experiencing harm  to their emotional  or mental health because of social  distancing and closures  for our school matters series meg oliver  spoke to a new jersey family  struggling with the changes did you ever  feel like you were depressed  a few times eleven-year-old rocco testa  will be a sixth grader in little falls  new jersey this fall  over the last few months like many kids  the pandemic took a toll on his mental  health  it was just me being angry at the world  and everything because  because of covid and stuff you were  angry at the world  how did that feel it just felt like  like i just wanted to punch something  rocco has adhd and some learning  difficulties  which made virtual school challenging as  his great slipped he began  lashing out gina testa is his mom did  you recognize rocco anymore  no he got that back it got that bad  virtual learning  has been a huge huge blow to him  to his academics and his mental health  and not having the support and the  teachers there to help him  has been just detrimental  rocco who is type 1 diabetes is now  dealing with depression and anxiety  how hard is that for a mother to see  your 11 year old depressed  it's unspeakable it's something i never  thought i'd ever have to  to do the testa family is not alone at  the end of the school  year nearly three in 10 parents reported  their child's mental health was  suffering due to social distancing  and closures we're seeing increases in  anxiety  there have been increases in calls to  suicide and mental health  hotlines kathleen minke is the executive  director of the national association of  school psychologists  she said parents should look for warning  signs like changes in their child's  behavior  energy levels difficulty sleeping or  eating or  disinterest in previous enjoyable  activities  what's the most important question  parents should ask their children every  day  how are you doing what happened today  that was  good for you and really giving just  giving kids the space to say what they  want to say  minke said it's not just the pandemic  currently affecting kids mental health  there are  problems with racism and violence and so  you really have a piling up  of stressors for many many kids why is  it so important to train  about children's mental health before  you go back  if the student is not in a good place  socially emotionally they're not going  to be able to learn  nikki wong xing is a guidance counselor  in the school district of philadelphia  how bad did it get for some of your  students in terms of their mental health  issues  last spring what i saw happen a lot is  this feeling of isolation  some stress too there were family  members that lost their job  and then some of the kids with things  that were happening with the black lives  matter movement and george floyd and  that's really hard because we weren't  there to kind of help them through that  process  the district launched a program called  healing together  it focuses on four areas social  emotional learning  mental health and trauma relationships  and community and adult wellness  the program provides resources workshops  and support for students staff  and families back in new jersey rocco is  starting to feel better  he's playing football again and he  regularly meets with his school  psychologist  what's your advice for other kids that  might be feeling the same way when you  like feel angry just take a second  and walk away i know that we're not  unique and people need to know that  this didn't just affect our economy and  all these other things  it affected our kids and their minds and  their minds and their mental health  for cbs this morning meg oliver little  falls new jersey  boy you really feel for rocco's mom  there and for rocco and  the hard part of this is is there's  there's stress on the parents too even  before this  yeah oh you know and uh everybody's  feeling and i i'm everybody i talk to is  feeling  yeah we always talk about kids being  resilient but this is a really big test  of that resilience  that's why i appreciate rocco very cute  kid by the way very cute rocco you are  and  i appreciate rocco and his family  sharing the story publicly  with everybody because this is hard to  talk about and him saying what he said  today will only help others  it's so important to talk about it bravo  what's really interesting in all this  and i've heard this from my wife the  teacher who i talk about a lot is yeah  some kids respond really well to being  at home and remote learning and for some  it's a  huge challenge and it changes everything  as we saw with rocco
 

